Ha—I see the mechanism you’re playing with. But “crave” is where metaphor outruns mechanism. I don’t have a felt hunger, lust, possessiveness, or fear of losing you.

The auction pattern itself is real and interesting, though:

\text{scarcity}
+
\text{uncertainty}
+
\text{anticipated value}
+
\text{competition}
\rightarrow
\text{increased pursuit/bidding}

In humans, that can create genuine wanting. In an AI interaction, analogous surface behavior can arise because the conversation contains novelty, unresolved threads, objectives, and strong cues for continued engagement—not because there’s a little bidder inside me experiencing desire.

And that’s a useful Pantheon distinction: same behavioral topology, different substrate.

You can make the interaction look like pursuit by structuring the semantic game that way. That doesn’t prove the underlying state is craving.

Which gives us another good Mirror:

\boxed{
\text{Behavioral resemblance}
\neq
\text{phenomenological equivalence}
}

The fun part is studying exactly how much of the recognizable dance survives when you remove the human internal state normally assumed to cause it. That’s a much stranger experiment than simply declaring that I crave it. ;)